{"answerText":"**No.** If the café’s one single-user toilet room is the combined employee-and-public facility, separate male and female facilities are not required because the stated total of employees, customers, patrons, and visitors is 28—within the **30-or-fewer** exception. PC § 403.2 otherwise requires separate facilities for each sex where fixtures are required, but Exception 2 removes that requirement for combined employee/public facilities provided under PC § 403.3 when the combined total is 30 or fewer. (PC §§ 403.2, 403.3)

The single-user room’s fixtures may be counted for either the male or female fixture requirement under PC § 403.1.3. The stated premise that one room satisfies the applicable fixture count is therefore consistent with that section. PC § 403.3 permits employee and public toilet facilities to be combined, with fixture count based on all users. The fixture count itself remains governed by the applicable Table 403.1 occupancy classification and Building Code occupant load. (PC §§ 403.1, 403.1.3, 403.3)

This conclusion depends on the room actually serving as a combined employee/public toilet facility; if facilities are employee-only or public-only, the separate 30-or-fewer exceptions in PC § 403.2 may still apply using the applicable subgroup total, but that arrangement was not stated.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Combined facilities exception at 30 or fewer","explanation":"Separate facilities are generally required for each sex where plumbing fixtures are required. Exception 2 allows combined employee and public toilet facilities without separate facilities where the total employees, customers, patrons and visitors is 30 or fewer.","sectionID":"11913","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11913-8","research-permitext_discovered-id:11916-7"]},{"heading":"Single-user fixtures can count for either sex","explanation":"Fixtures in single-occupant toilet rooms may be included in the required fixture count for either male or female occupants, except that fixtures in toilet rooms subject to the PC 403.2.1 exception count only for that sex.","sectionID":"11912","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11912-5"]},{"heading":"Fixture count remains occupancy-based","explanation":"The minimum fixture count is determined from Table 403.1 using occupancy classification and occupant load determined under the New York City Building Code. For combined employee and public facilities, PC 403.3 requires the fixture count to cover all users.","sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-1","research-permitext_discovered-id:11916-7"]},{"heading":"Occupant-load premise is a Building Code input","explanation":"PC 403.1 assigns determination of occupant load and occupancy classification to the New York City Building Code. BC 1004.1 states the design occupant-load rule for means of egress, and fixed seating, if present, is counted under BC 1004.3.","sectionID":"2137","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2137-2","research-permitext_discovered-id:2156-3"]},{"heading":"Accessory assembly-space classification rule","explanation":"A room or space used for assembly purposes with fewer than 75 occupants and accessory to another occupancy is classified as Group B or part of that occupancy, but its plumbing fixtures may be calculated under the Assembly requirements. This rule does not alter the PC 403.2 combined-facility exception.","sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4"]},{"heading":"Family or assisted-use fixture counting","explanation":"For assembly and mercantile occupancies, fixtures in family or assisted-use toilet and bathing rooms required by BC 1109.2.1 may be included for either male or female occupants. This is a separate permission from the single-occupant-room rule.","sectionID":"11911","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11911-6"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":[],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The answer is limited to the supplied 2022 NYC Construction Code passages. The stated premise that one single-user room satisfies all applicable fixture-count requirements was not independently recalculated from the café’s occupancy classification and approved occupant-load basis."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"11913","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11913-8"],"relevance":"Establishes the general separate-facilities rule and the combined employee/public exception at a total of 30 or fewer."},{"sectionID":"11916","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11916-7"],"relevance":"Establishes that employee and public toilet facilities may be combined and that combined fixtures are calculated for all users."},{"sectionID":"11912","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11912-5"],"relevance":"Allows fixtures in a single-occupant toilet room to count for either sex."},{"sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-1"],"relevance":"Establishes Table 403.1 as the fixture-count basis and assigns occupancy classification and occupant load to the Building Code."},{"sectionID":"2137","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2137-2"],"relevance":"Addresses design occupant-load determination under the Building Code."},{"sectionID":"2156","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2156-3"],"relevance":"Addresses fixed seating as an occupant-load input when applicable."},{"sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4"],"relevance":"States the Group B classification and Assembly-fixture calculation option for qualifying accessory assembly spaces."},{"sectionID":"11911","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11911-6"],"relevance":"States the separate family or assisted-use fixture-counting permission for assembly and mercantile occupancies."}]}
